Weather Rescue Brazil — licensing
=================================

Four different things live in this repository. As of 2026-09-17 all of them
are open, and the reasoning for each is written down below.

1. THE OBSERVATIONS ARE NOT OURS TO LICENCE.

   The measurements themselves come from nineteenth-century Brazilian
   government publications and are in the public domain (Lei 9.610; the corpus
   is 1882-1890). Anyone may use the numbers themselves for any purpose. No
   licence here restricts that, and no licence could.

2. THE TRANSCRIPTIONS, VERDICTS AND ANNOTATIONS — CC0 1.0 (public domain
   dedication)

   What this project added: reading the printed tables into machine-readable
   values, the per-row quality verdicts, the corrections, and the provenance.

   To the extent any rights exist in them, they are waived. Use them for
   anything, commercial or not, with or without credit.

   https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode

   WHY A WAIVER RATHER THAN A LICENCE. A faithful, exhaustive transcription of
   a printed public-domain table exercises no creative selection — the choice
   of what to record and how to arrange it was the 1880s printer's. Lei 9.610
   art. 7º §2º is explicit that protection "não abarca os dados ou materiais em
   si mesmos", and §3º that in the sciences it does not reach "o seu conteúdo
   científico ou técnico". United States law reaches the same place through
   Feist. Asserting a licence over these numbers would claim a right that
   probably does not exist; CC0 says so honestly and removes the doubt for
   anyone downstream.

   Credit is not required. It is still welcome, and the attribution string
   below is the one the digitising collection asks for.

3. THE CODE — Apache License 2.0

   Everything in `src/`, `scripts/`, `tests/` and `profiles/`. Full text in
   LICENSE-CODE.

   https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

4. THE MODEL WEIGHTS — Apache License 2.0

   The LoRA adapters and the packaged quantised model, under the same terms as
   the code and the same terms as the base model they adapt (Qwen3.5-2B,
   Apache-2.0).

   Read MODEL_CARD.md before building on these: the training labels were
   generated by the Gemini API, and while Google claims no ownership of
   generated content, anyone redistributing the weights should know where the
   targets came from.

ATTRIBUTION
-----------

   Acervo: Biblioteca Digital de Obras Raras do Observatório Nacional, via
   DocVirt. Digitalizado e transcrito pelo projeto Weather Rescue Brazil.

The collection asks for this string and it travels with every downloaded page
in `data/raw/docvirt/*/*.json`. Please carry it — this is a request from the
archive that digitised the pages, and it is separate from, and survives, the
CC0 dedication above.

The page SCANS are not redistributed by this project. They remain with the
digitising collection under whatever terms that collection sets.

A NOTE ON WHY THIS CHANGED
--------------------------

Until 2026-09-17 the transcriptions, the weights and the code were
non-commercial (CC BY-NC 4.0 and PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0). The intent was
that the work stay free for everyone and not be resold. Two things changed
that.

The first is that non-commercial terms did not do what they were meant to do.
On the numbers they asserted a right that probably does not exist (see §2), and
the previous version of this file said in one paragraph that "anyone may use
the numbers themselves for any purpose" and in the next that those same numbers
were CC BY-NC. That contradiction was real and it was ours.

The second is what it cost. A non-commercial licence is a hard bar at the
International Surface Pressure Databank, Scientific Data, Dryad, Figshare,
Brazilian federal open data (Resolução CGINDA nº 2/2017 enumerates CC0, PDDL,
CC BY and ODbL) and, in practice, PANGAEA. At Copernicus C3S it does not
disqualify a deposit but it lowers its ingestion priority, which is worse,
because nothing tells you. On the code it ruled out Geoscientific Model
Development and JOSS, both of which require an OSI-conforming licence — and it
meant the national meteorological institutes who might run this pipeline on
their own archives could not.

The project's goal is that the observations reach the reanalyses and that the
method be reusable by anyone with a shelf of scanned weather tables. Every
restriction here was working against that goal, and none of them was protecting
anything worth protecting.
